<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miguel Hidalgo y Costilla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1753–1811</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Miguel Hidalgo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miguel Hidalgo y Costilla was a Mexican Catholic priest who began the war of independence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. On 16 September 1810, in the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolores-hidalgo">Dolores</a>, he called on his parishioners to rise, an event known as the Grito de Dolores. His mass army of peasants and Indigenous people took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a> but was defeated at the Bridge of Calderón in 1811; he was captured and executed. He is honoured as the father of Mexican independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla.jpg" alt="Miguel Hidalgo y Costilla" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joaquín Ramírez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General%C3%ADsimo_Miguel_Hidalgo_y_Costilla.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
